From: SkiffOS <skiffos@skiffos.com>
Date: Thu, 16 Apr 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm: arm926ejs: sun5i: flush D-cache before disabling I-cache with settle

cleanup_before_linux() on arm926ejs disables I-cache then D-cache:

  icache_disable();   <- instruction fetches now go through AHB/MBUS
  dcache_disable();   <- flush_dcache_all() writes dirty D-cache lines
                         to DRAM via MBUS: large write burst

With I-cache disabled, instruction fetches for flush_dcache_all() and
for the subsequent udelay() settle in boot_jump_linux() go through
AHB/MBUS.  The MBUS arbiter deadlocks: it is still processing the
dirty D-cache write burst while the CPU issues a new MBUS read (fetch).
Result: CPU stalls indefinitely.  No output appears after
"Starting kernel..." even with a 1-second udelay in boot_jump_linux().

Fix: reverse the disable order for sun5i.

  dcache_disable();   <- flush_dcache_all() write burst; I-cache still ON
                         -> instruction fetches still served from I-cache
  DSB;                <- drain write buffer to AHB
  udelay(500000);     <- 500 ms: reads TIMER MMIO (APB, not DRAM),
                         I-cache on -> no MBUS read pressure -> MBUS drains
  icache_disable();   <- MBUS drained; first uncached fetch won't stall

Why udelay() and not a DRAM busy-wait loop:
  A raw "for (_s = N; _s; _s--)" reads _s from stack (DRAM) on every
  iteration.  If the MBUS write buffer is still in flight, that first
  read stalls indefinitely — making the settle useless.
  udelay() reads the hardware TIMER MMIO register (APB bus, different
  AHB slave from DRAM), so it does not stall on MBUS DRAM contention.

Debug markers (TEMPORARY — remove once boot progresses past this point):
  [LINUX-DBG] cleanup: pre-dcache-flush   <- printed while D+I caches on (safe)
  [LINUX-DBG] cleanup: post-settle        <- printed after 500ms settle, before
                                             icache_disable (I-cache still on;
                                             MBUS should be drained; D-cache off
                                             so format string read goes to DRAM
                                             via MBUS — safe only if settle worked)

If "pre-dcache-flush" prints but "post-settle" does not: hang is inside
dcache_disable() (flush_dcache_all) or the settle mechanism itself.

If "post-settle" prints: cleanup_before_linux completes; the hang is in
the kernel decompressor (kernel patches 0003-0008 need more investigation).

Signed-off-by: SkiffOS <skiffos@skiffos.com>
---
 arch/arm/cpu/arm926ejs/cpu.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

--- a/arch/arm/cpu/arm926ejs/cpu.c
+++ b/arch/arm/cpu/arm926ejs/cpu.c
@@ -17,6 +17,9 @@
 #include <cpu_func.h>
 #include <irq_func.h>
 #include <asm/cache.h>
+#ifdef CONFIG_MACH_SUN5I
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#endif
 #include <asm/system.h>

 static void cache_flush(void);
@@ -43,12 +46,33 @@
 	 * we turn off caches etc ...
 	 */

-	disable_interrupts();
-
-
-	/* turn off I/D-cache */
-	icache_disable();
-	dcache_disable();
+	disable_interrupts();
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_MACH_SUN5I
+	/*
+	 * sun5i AHB/MBUS stall: dcache_disable() calls flush_dcache_all()
+	 * which creates a large DRAM write burst (all dirty D-cache lines).
+	 * If I-cache is disabled first, subsequent instruction fetches for
+	 * the flush loop and for any udelay() settle go through AHB/MBUS
+	 * and stall on the pending DRAM writes — the MBUS arbiter deadlocks.
+	 *
+	 * Fix: flush D-cache first while I-cache is still on.  I-cache
+	 * supplies instruction fetches; udelay() reads TIMER MMIO (APB,
+	 * not DRAM) so it does not stall on MBUS contention.  After 500 ms
+	 * the MBUS write buffer is drained; then disable I-cache — the
+	 * first uncached fetch hits a drained MBUS.
+	 */
+	printf("[LINUX-DBG] cleanup: pre-dcache-flush\n");
+	dcache_disable();   /* flush_dcache_all() + clear C bit: MBUS write burst */
+	asm volatile("mcr p15, 0, %0, c7, c10, 4" :: "r"(0) : "memory"); /* DSB */
+	udelay(2000000);    /* 2000 ms: I-cache on -> MBUS drains via TIMER MMIO reads */
+	printf("[LINUX-DBG] cleanup: post-settle\n");
+	icache_disable();   /* MBUS drained — first uncached fetch will not stall */
+#else
+	/* turn off I/D-cache */
+	icache_disable();
+	dcache_disable();
+#endif
 	l2_cache_disable();

 	/* flush I/D-cache */
